    {
      "sectionId": "section-hobgoblin-versions",
      "heading": "Hobgoblin Versions",
      "sourceAnchor": "hobgoblin-versions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-hobgoblin-versions-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Hobgoblin has three versions. The original version was published in\n\tVolo’s Guide to Monsters. Their signature traits were Martial Training and\n\tSaving Face."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-hobgoblin-versions-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The introduction of the custom origin rules gave use the second version of\n\tthe Hobgoblin. This provided the ability to make a hobgoblin who was actually\n\twell-suited to martial classes and also allowed you to trade weapon\n\tproficiencies down to tool proficiencies if that’s something that you\n\twanted."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-hobgoblin-versions-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Most recently, Monsters of the Multiverse published a reworked version of the\n\tHobgoblin with reimagined traits. Fortune of the Many is essentially a weaker\n\tversion of Saving Face, but otherwise there’s little mechanical overlap with\n\tthe older version of the Hobgoblin. The new version also introduces Fey Gift,\n\twhich encourages you to use the Help action in combat."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-hobgoblins-in-the-2024-dnd-rules",
      "heading": "Hobgoblins in the 2024 DnD Rules",
      "sourceAnchor": "hobgoblins-in-the-2024-dnd-rules",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-hobgoblins-in-the-2024-dnd-rules-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Hobgoblins were most recently updated in Monsters of the Multiverse, and have not yet been updated for the 2024 DnD rules. To play a Hobgoblin in the 2024 DnD rules, ignore the Ability Score Increases described in Monsters of the Multiverse. Instead, use the Ability Score Increases provided by your Background in the 2024 rules."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-hobgoblins-in-the-2024-dnd-rules-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The MotM Hobgoblin’s ability to use Help as a Bonus Action became very powerful with the publication of Forgotten Realms: Heroes of Faerun. Several new feats, such as "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Harper Agent",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/2024-dnd/feats/harper-agent/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", offer additional rider effects on the Help Action. By combining those feats with the Hobgoblins’ Hospitality trait, you can have a huge impact with a single Bonus Action."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Hobgoblin Classes (MMoM)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-mmom",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Between Fortune of the Many and Flash of Genius, you’re very well-equipped to recover from failed rolls. It may be difficult to keep Fey Gift available because artificers nearly always have a use for their Bonus Action (homunculus, etc.)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Many barbarian subclasses have a way to use their Bonus Action every turn while raging, but they may not be as impactful as Fey Gift so skipping your subclass’s Bonus Action for a few rounds per day won’t kill you. If nothing else, the Hospitality option’s temporary hit points are a good layer of additional protection, especially once you’re raging and have damage resistance. Fortune of the Many can help you succeed on difficult saving throws, especially mental ones which often take barbarians out of a fight."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fey Gift feels like a natural extension of the Bard’s existing support options. While you already have Bardic Inspiration, it has a limited number of uses per rest, so you can use Fey Gift as an additional pool of support. However, needing to get within 5 feet of an enemy to Help attack them is a bad place for most bards to be."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The go-to use for the Cleric’s option at basically every level is Spiritual Weapon. Outside of combat, you can use the Hospitality option to get some temporary hit points while helping an ally with something like a skill check."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Many varieties of druids have few uses for their Bonus Action, so it’s often left open, leaving room for Fey Gift. The biggest challenge is that you need to be within 5 feet of a creature in order to Help your ally attack it, and that’s a terrible place for most druids to be. Circle of the Moon is probably your best option since you generally won’t use your Bonus Action once you’re in Wild Shape."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Most fighters don’t use their Bonus Action every turn, and if you’re built for melee you’re going to be constantly in melee where you can Help your allies attack a creature. Fortune of the Many provides some insurance against hard saving throws until Indomitable comes online at level 9, and even once you have Indomitable you can still combine it with Fortune of the Many."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Monks use their Bonus Action every turn in combat. Fey Gift’s Hospitality option provides temporary hit points that you can activate before going into combat, but I don’t think that’s good enough. Similarly, Fortune of the Many provides a helpful bonus to saves, but saves aren’t the source of the Monk’s frailty problems."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Much like the Fighter, the Paladin doesn’t use their Bonus Action every turn (though many of their spells are cast as a Bonus Action), so Fey Gift is easy to bring into play in combat and giving you a support option that doesn’t require hitting something. Fortune of the Many provides an additional layer on top of Aura of Protection, making you exceptionally durable."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rangers use their Bonus Action heavily, making Fey Gift difficult to use in combat. The Hospitality option works fine, but if that’s all you want, play a variant human and take Eldritch Adept (Fiendish Vigor)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rogues use their Bonus Action for Cunning Action, two-weapon fighting, and sometimes for subclass features. You may find times to fit Fey Gift into your turn, but considering that the Rogue wants Advantage to make Sneak Attack work, it makes more sense for someone else in the party to bring this in order to help you."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fey Gift becomes purely a non-combat support option."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fey Gift could work for hexblades in the same way that it works for fighters and paladins, but other warlocks will have trouble using it in combat."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-mmom",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fey Gift becomes purely a non-combat support option. If you want to Help in combat, get an owl familiar."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Hobgoblin Classes (Customizable Origins)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-custom-origin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-custom-origin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the next section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Saving Face is the only noteworthy thing that the Hobgoblin brings to the Artificer. Sure, it’s a good trait and it stacks with Flash of Genius, but that’s really not enough. Trade the weapon proficiencies for more tools if you feel compelled to do so."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Saving Face is the only noteworthy trait, and while it’s nice it’s not really enough."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Saving Face is the only noteworthy trait, and while it’s nice it’s not really enough."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Even if your domain doesn’t provide martial weapon proficiency, two weapon proficiencies aren’t going to make you suddenly good with weapons. Stick to cantrips. Of course, that means that Saving Face is the only noteworthy trait here."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Even druids who use weapons are going to use Shillelagh, so the proficiencies are wasted, and Saving Face isn’t enough to carry the race."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Saving Face is the only noteworthy trait, and while it’s nice it’s not really enough."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The martial weapon proficiencies barely matter, and if you want martial weapons as a monk you can either play a kensei or use the Dedication Weapon Optional Class Feature. Armor proficiency on a monk is useless. That just leaves Saving Face, and again, it’s not enough to carry the race."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Saving Face is the only noteworthy trait, and while it’s nice it’s not really enough."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Saving Face is the only noteworthy trait, and while it’s nice it’s not really enough."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Saving Face is the only noteworthy trait, and while it’s nice it’s not really enough."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Light armor and Saving Face provide some much-needed durability, and you could use Saving Face to rescue high-value spell attacks, especially at low levels when your spell slots are severely limited. Mage Armor can replace light armor (and provides better AC), but with the Sorcerer’s strictly limited pool of spells known, freeing one for other things is very tempting."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Saving Face is the only noteworthy trait, and while it’s nice it’s not really enough."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Light armor and Saving Face provide some much-needed durability. Wizards are less dependent on spell attacks at low levels than sorcerers, and can spare a prepared spell for Mage Armor much more easily than the Sorcerer, so the benefits of the Hobgoblin’s traits aren’t as useful for the Wizard as they are for the Sorcerer."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Hobgoblin Classes (Classic Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-default",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-default-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "not"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " using the option “Customizing Your Origin” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything or the updated version of the race published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. If you are using those rules, scroll up to the previous section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "An Intelligence increase is all that the Artificer really needs, but\n\tproficiency in two martial weapons is nice for Battlesmith builds that want to\n\texplore weapons before their subclass comes online."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Barbarians are too dependent on Strength to work without a Strength increase,\n\tand Saving Face isn’t enough to fix that."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Nothing useful for the Bard."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Nothing useful for the Cleric."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Nothing useful for the Druid."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Constitution and Intelligence could work for an Eldritch Knight, but without\n\ta Strength/Dexterity increase you’ll lag offensively. Saving Face will help,\n\tbut not enough."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
